{
  "dom_id": "step-q118165",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14186732/assets/23ae569de9ed72a8a2579c4a67eac48ae530aa89f19503e3b5d7ab8fb6ca6e80.png)\n\nIn the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown above, find $x.$",
  "worked_solution": "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {127}^{∘}+(2x)+(2x+{18}^{∘})+{110}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘}) & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{260}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={280}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
